questions.question:
Describe the family Poxviridae under the following headings:
A(i) classification;
A(ii) morphology and structure;
A(iii) genome;
A(iv) replication and maturation.
B(i) Explain the pathogenesis of smallpox (variola virus).
B(ii) Explain the host range and tissue tropism.
C(i) Distinguish smallpox virus from monkeypox virus.
C(ii) Distinguish the clinical forms of smallpox.

Describe the family Orthomyxoviridae under the following headings:
A(i) classification (genera and the hosts each genus infects);
A(ii) morphology and structure of the virion, including the surface glycoproteins;
A(iii) genome type and organisation;
A(iv) replication cycle.
B(i) Explain the basis of the tropism of influenza virus for the respiratory tract.
B(ii) Explain how the virus produces local respiratory injury and spreads within the respiratory tract.
C(i) Distinguish between influenza A, B and C in antigenic change and disease.
C(ii) Explain the nature and significance of the antigenic changes for host immunity.

Describe the family Retroviridae under the following headings:
A(i) classification;
A(ii) morphology and structure;
A(iii) genome and genes;
A(iv) replication cycle.
B(i) Explain the basis of the tropism and pathogenesis of HIV.
B(ii) Explain the host range of retroviruses.
C(i) Distinguish HIV-1 from HIV-2.
C(ii) Distinguish HIV from the human T-cell leukaemia viruses (HTLV).

Describe the human hepatitis viruses under the following headings:
A(i) classification;
A(ii) morphology and structure;
A(iii) genome;
A(iv) replication cycle.
B(i) Explain the basis of pathogenesis and liver damage.
B(ii) Explain the host range and animal reservoirs.
C(i) Distinguish hepatitis A virus from hepatitis E virus.
C(ii) Distinguish HDV co-infection from HDV superinfection.

Describe the family Herpesviridae under the following headings:
A(i) classification;
A(ii) morphology and structure;
A(iii) genome;
A(iv) replication cycle (herpes simplex virus).
B(i) Explain the tissue tropism and latency of each group.
B(ii) Explain the pathogenesis of HSV, VZV, CMV and EBV.
C(i) Distinguish between herpes simplex virus types 1 and 2.
C(ii) Distinguish between varicella and zoster.

Describe the family Arenaviridae under the following headings:
A(i) classification;
A(ii) morphology and structure;
A(iii) genome;
A(iv) replication cycle.
B(i) Explain the pathogenesis and tissue tropism of Lassa virus.
B(ii) Explain the host range, reservoirs and transmission.
C(i) Distinguish the Old World from the New World arenaviruses.
C(ii) Distinguish Lassa virus from lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus.

Describe the family Flaviviridae under the following headings:
A(i) classification (genera and members);
A(ii) morphology and structure;
A(iii) genome;
A(iv) replication cycle.
B(i) Explain the pathogenesis and tissue tropism of tick-borne encephalitis virus, dengue virus, West Nile virus and hepatitis C virus.
B(ii) Explain the host range, vectors and reservoirs.
C(i) Distinguish the genus Flavivirus from the genus Hepacivirus.
C(ii) Distinguish dengue virus from Zika virus.

Describe the family Adenoviridae under the following headings:
A(i) classification;
A(ii) morphology and structure;
A(iii) genome;
A(iv) replication cycle.
B(i) Explain the pathogenesis and tissue tropism of adenoviruses.
B(ii) Explain the tropism and host-cell requirements of parvoviruses.
C(i) Distinguish adenoviruses from parvoviruses.
C(ii) Distinguish the clinical disease and management of the two groups.

A. Describe innate immunity under the following headings:
A(i) definition and components;
A(ii) the skin as an external barrier;
A(iii) mucous membranes as external barriers;
A(iv) chemical barriers;
A(v) cellular defence.
B.
B(i) Distinguish innate from adaptive immunity.

A. Describe the urinary bladder under the following headings:
A(i) parts and epithelium;
A(ii) the detrusor muscle;
A(iii) the trigone;
A(iv) the urethra.
B.
B(i) Explain why intravesical pressure rises very little until the bladder is relatively full.
B(ii) Distinguish between the internal and external urinary sphincters.

A. Describe the sequence of events from a motor nerve action potential to skeletal muscle contraction and relaxation:
A(i) events at the neuromuscular junction;
A(ii) the sarcotubular system and calcium release;
A(iii) the contractile and regulatory proteins;
A(iv) the cross-bridge cycle and relaxation.
B.
B(i) State the abnormalities of muscle and its contraction.
B(ii) State how transmission at the neuromuscular junction can be altered.

A. Describe the penis under the following headings:
A(i) erectile columns and erectile tissue;
A(ii) arterial supply;
A(iii) venous drainage;
A(iv) sensory and motor supply.
B.
B(i) Explain the mechanism of erection, including the stimuli, neural pathway and mediators.
B(ii) Distinguish the role of the dorsal artery from that of the deep artery.

A. Describe the menstrual cycle under the following headings:
A(i) definition and length;
A(ii) follicular phase;
A(iii) ovulation;
A(iv) luteal phase.
B.
B(i) Describe the three phases of the uterine cycle and the hormones responsible.
B(ii) Explain why multiple ovulations do not occur in a cycle.

A. Describe the types and structure of muscle under the following headings:
A(i) the general properties and the three types;
A(ii) organisation of skeletal muscle from whole muscle to filament;
A(iii) smooth muscle;
A(iv) cardiac muscle;
A(v) skeletal muscle fibre types.
B.
B(i) Explain the functional differences between slow and fast fibres.
B(ii) Distinguish between isometric and isotonic contraction.
